## Architecture Decisions

- **Layered architecture** (Handler → Service → Repository) with strict
  unidirectional dependencies enforced by Go's `internal/` package system.

- **Cache-aside pattern** with Redis. Cache misses fall through gracefully
  to the TMDB API. Cache errors are non-fatal — the app serves live data
  if Redis is unavailable.

- **Connection pooling** for both PostgreSQL (pgxpool) and Redis to handle
  concurrent requests without exhausting connections.

- **Parameterized SQL queries** throughout — no string interpolation,
  no SQL injection surface.

- **Context propagation** on all I/O operations for timeout and
  cancellation support.

- **Aware of cache stampede** problem — current implementation is suitable
  for low-to-medium traffic; production mitigation would use distributed
  locking (e.g. Redlock).

## Known Limitations & Production Considerations

- **Ban enforcement:** Banning a user invalidates future logins but does
  not revoke existing JWTs. Production solution: maintain a Redis token
  blacklist keyed by JWT ID (jti claim), checked in RequireAuth middleware.
  TTL matches the token's remaining lifetime.

## Admin Setup

The admin account is created separately from the API server using
a dedicated seeder script. The API server never holds admin credentials.

# First time setup
make seed-admin email=admin@yourdomain.com password=YourStrongPassword

# Rotate admin password
make seed-admin email=admin@yourdomain.com password=NewPassword --force

## Security Model

**Logout** is server-side. On `POST /auth/logout`, the token's unique
ID (jti) is stored in Redis with a TTL equal to the token's remaining
lifetime. Every protected request checks this blacklist — revoked tokens
are rejected immediately, not just after expiry.

**Ban enforcement** is real-time. When an admin bans a user, a Redis
marker is written instantly. Every subsequent request from that user
is rejected at the middleware layer, even if their JWT is still
cryptographically valid.

**Fail behaviour** — Redis errors on security checks fail closed
(request rejected). Redis errors on the movie cache fail open
(live data served). Security optimisations and performance
optimisations have opposite failure modes by design.